---
Part III, Chapter 3: Helix
Language: Prose
---

# Helix

Helix needs no extension. It learns a language from its configuration, and one command sets Loom up there, with everything Supported editors describes:

```bash
loom helix
```

## What it needs

`loom helix` builds Loom's grammar for Helix, and building it takes a C compiler, the same one Helix uses for its own grammars. On macOS that's the Xcode Command Line Tools, and on Linux your distribution's build tools.

The grammar comes with the `loom` command, so it always matches the Loom you have installed. Run `loom helix` again whenever you upgrade Loom, and it rebuilds the grammar from the new release. The command arrived after Loom 0.0.11, so you need a newer release.

## What it changes

`loom helix` writes to Helix's configuration folder, `~/.config/helix` on macOS and Linux, and nowhere else. It puts three things there:

- the compiled grammar, at `runtime/grammars/loom.so`;
- the queries that tell Helix what to colour, in `runtime/queries/loom`;
- a block of two tables in `languages.toml`.

The block names the `.loom` extension and starts the language server with `loom lsp`:

```toml
# Loom: written by `loom helix`, which replaces this block on every run
[[language]]
name = "loom"
scope = "source.loom"
file-types = ["loom"]
roots = [".loom"]
injection-regex = "^loom$"
language-servers = ["loom"]

[language-server.loom]
command = "loom"
args = ["lsp", "--stdio"]
# Loom: end
```

A rerun replaces the block between its two comment lines and leaves the rest of the file as you wrote it. If the file already sets Loom up by hand, outside the block, `loom helix` changes nothing and tells you so: remove your own Loom entries, and run it again. To undo the setup, delete the block and the two runtime entries.

## Colours

Check the setup with `$ hx --health loom`. The tree-sitter parser and the highlight queries should both have a tick. Textobject and indent queries have a cross: Loom ships neither, and Helix works fine without them.

Then open `$ hx corpus/hello.loom`. Loom's marks take colours of their own: headings take your theme's heading colour, the `[hello.ts]` sink reads as a path, `=>` and `~` read as operators, and `::[Greeting function]` reads as a label. The prose between them is Markdown, and the code in each section is coloured in its own language. The first section gets TypeScript from its sink, `hello.ts`, and the second from the frontmatter's `Language: TypeScript`.

Helix colours a section in the language Loom would give it, with one gap. A section with no sink and no `{Lang}` takes the language the frontmatter names, and when the frontmatter names none, Loom falls back to the project's `primary` language. Helix can't read the project's settings, so it leaves that section's code plain. Write `Language` into the frontmatter, and the code takes its colour.

## Checks and navigation

In Helix, `gd` on an anchor jumps to the section it names, `space k` shows the type under the cursor, and `space d` lists every problem in the file, Loom's own mistakes included.

TypeScript's checks need TypeScript in the project. Turn it on with `$ loom add typescript`, then add a TypeScript for the server to check with, `$ bun add -d typescript@6`. It has to be TypeScript 6 or earlier. TypeScript 7 is a native program with no JavaScript API for the server to load, so the server passes it over.
